This research focuses on enhancing Visual Association Detection (VAD), a task that has seen limited progress due to the presence of false leads in training data. These false associations, which are incorrectly labeled positive connections between objects, have not been previously addressed. Our work makes the following contributions:
1) We introduce a novel, object-centric analysis of the VAD dataset to pinpoint the source of false positives: a) weak associations, where the given location pair is incorrect due to an object being present near the query target's centroid; and and b) accidental associations, also stemming from the presence of an object in close proximity to the query target's centroid. A statistical model of the VAD data is established to quantify the impact of these issues on task performance;
2) A new strategy, Visual Association Denoising (VAD-Denoise), is proposed to address these problems by automatically filtering out, reassigning, or using additional annotations for the associations affected by these false positives. Our model is assessed on the VAD dataset and shows significant performance gains compared to the current state-of-the-art methods when applied to 2D-3D, 3D-3D associative relationships.

